From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Date: Sun, 21 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] riscv32i: dmem drives two procedural signals, so declare them
 as regs

dmem.v assigns we_mem and mem_out inside always @* blocks while
declaring we_mem as a wire and leaving mem_out an implicit net. A net
cannot be the target of a procedural assignment (IEEE 1364-2005 6.1.2,
IEEE 1800-2023 6.5): the signals are variables and the declarations say
otherwise. yosys's own Verilog frontend accepts it and infers the
variable; slang does not, and reports

    dmem.v:41:10: error: cannot assign to a net within a procedural context
    dmem.v:62:16: error: cannot assign to a net within a procedural context

nine of them, five on we_mem and four on mem_out, so the design cannot
be read by any flow that synthesises with slang. bazel-orfs synthesises
every flow with slang.

The fix is the declarations, not the code: `reg [3:0] we_mem` and
`output reg [31:0] mem_out`, which is what both frontends were already
building. The elaborated design is unchanged -- same always blocks, same
drivers, both case statements still full -- and the design synthesises
and routes as before on either frontend.

Not upstreamed; carried by bazel-orfs. Retires at a //:bump onto an ORFS
whose riscv32i declares these two signals as variables.
---
--- a/flow/designs/src/riscv32i/dmem.v
+++ b/flow/designs/src/riscv32i/dmem.v
@@ -13,10 +13,10 @@
    input 	 r_w;
    input [31:0]  mem_addr;
    input [31:0]  mem_data;
-   output [31:0] mem_out;
+   output reg [31:0] mem_out;
    wire [1:0] 	 sel_mem;
    wire [3:0] 	 ce_mem;
-   wire [3:0] 	 we_mem;
+   reg [3:0] 	 we_mem;
    wire [31:0]   inter_dmem0;
    wire [31:0]   inter_dmem1;
    wire [31:0]   inter_dmem2;
